window.SEARCH=[{"t":"Home","u":"index.html","x":"A step-by-step guide Build Clautverse from scratch Start with an empty Nuxt 4 project. Finish with a working fan-community backend (accounts, communities, posts, missions, moderation) and a live page where you can try every API route. No experience needed Every idea is explained in plain words first, then in programming terms. If you can type and follow steps, you can follow this. Real code only Every code box is copied from the real project when this site is built. Nothing is simplified behind your back. Checkpoints Each chapter ends with something you can run and see, so you always know you "},{"t":"The big picture","u":"00-big-picture.html","x":"Before typing anything, get the map. This chapter has no code to write. It explains what we are building and gives names to the pieces so the later chapters feel familiar. What Clautverse is # In plain words Clautverse is a place for fans of public figures. Think of a reality-TV housemate. Their fans can join a community for that person, read what the person posts, do small missions to earn points, RSVP to watch-together events, and get notified when something happens. Staff ( admins ) approve who counts as a real \"Star\", handle reports about bad behaviour, and can hide a community. We are building the backend : the part that stores everything and enforces the rules. It has no pretty screens. Instead it has an API , which is a list of web addresses that other programs (a phone app, a website) can ask for things. The only web page we build is the API Explorer , a tool for trying those addresses by hand. The words you will see everywhere # Skim this list now and come back whenever a word feels fuzzy. The glossary has more. Word In plain words In programming terms Client / server The client asks (a browser, an app). The server answers. A client sends an HTTP request; the server program returns an HTTP response. API A menu of things a program can ask our server to do. A set of HTTP endpoints, here returning JSON. Endpoint / route One item on that menu, such as \"list communities\". A method plus a path, e.g. GET /api/communities . GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE Read, create, replace, change a little, remove. HTTP methods (verbs). GET never changes data. Status code A number saying how it went: 200 fine, 404 not found, 403 not allowed. The three-digit HTTP response code. 2xx success, 4xx the caller's fault, 5xx ours. JSON A tidy way to write data as text: {\"name\": \"Ada\"} . JavaScript Object Notation, the format our API speaks. Database, table, row A giant organised filing cabinet. A table is one drawer, a row is one card. PostgreSQL stores data in tables made of rows and columns. SQL The language for asking the database things. Structured Query Language. We write it through Drizzle instead of by hand most of the time. Migration A saved, numbered instruction for changing the database layout. A SQL file generated from schema changes and applied in order. Session / cookie A wristband you get at sign-in so you are recognised next time. A random token stored server-side, sent back by the browser in a cookie. Environment variable A setting kept outside the code, such as a password. A key/value the process reads at startup ( process.env , .env file). Transaction \"Do all these steps or none of them.\" A database transaction: atomic changes that commit together or roll back. The words specific to Clautverse # Fan : a normal signed-in user. Star : a public figure. The code calls this a talent (table talent , routes under /api/talents ). Same thing, two names. Category : a kind of fandom, such as Reality TV. Only active categories can be chosen. Community (fandom): the home of one Star's fans. One Star owns exactly one community. Originals : the first 100 people to join a community get a permanent numbered spot, 1 to 100. Leave or get removed and your number is retired forever. Lane : one of six labels a fan picks when joining ( rider , fandom_builder , builder , analyst , creator , mutual ). Drop : a post by the Star. It can be public, members-only, or Originals-only. Mission and XP : a task a Star sets, and the points a fan earns by completing it. XP is recorded in a ledger (a list of every change), never edited. Badge : a collectable award given by admins. Watch party : a scheduled event with an external link and an \"I'm going\" button. Report and block : how fans flag content and hide other users. The tools, and why each one # Tool Job Why this one Nuxt 4 The framework. It runs our server code and our one web page. Its server engine (Nitro) turns each file in server/api into an endpoint with no setup. Vue powers the web page. TypeScript JavaSc"},{"t":"Install tools and create the project","u":"01-setup.html","x":"In this chapter you install the tools and create an empty Nuxt 4 project. By the end you will see Nuxt's welcome page in your browser. Install the tools # Tip No Docker? You can install PostgreSQL directly instead. See PostgreSQL without Docker for installs and drop-in replacements for every Docker command. The guide uses PostgreSQL 17 . Tool What it is Check it Node.js 24 or newer Runs JavaScript outside a browser. Our server is a Node program. node -v pnpm Downloads and manages the libraries (packages) our project uses. pnpm -v Docker Desktop Runs the PostgreSQL database in a sealed box on your machine. docker -v Git Saves the history of your work. git --version In plain words A terminal is a window where you type instructions instead of clicking. On a Mac open \"Terminal\"; on Windows open \"PowerShell\" (or use WSL). Every line in a grey command box below is something you type there, then press Enter. In programming terms The project's package.json declares \"engines\": {\"node\": \">=24\"} . That means \"this project is built and tested on Node 24 or newer\". Nuxt itself documents a lower minimum, but follow the project's number so everything in this guide behaves the same for you. Install Node from nodejs.org (pick the current or LTS version that is 24 or higher) and Docker from docker.com . Then install pnpm: Open Docker Desktop once so it is running in the background. You will need it in chapter 4. Create the Nuxt 4 project # Nuxt's official command asks a few questions and then downloads a starter. This is the command from the Nuxt 4 installation page : Answer the questions like this: Template: minimal . It is the emptiest starting point. Package manager: pnpm . Modules: choose none. Do not add Docus, Nuxt UI or anything else. We add exactly what we need by hand, so you understand every piece. Git: yes, initialise a repository. Tip Prefer no questions? Use the flagged form from Nuxt's docs: npm create nuxt@latest clautverse -- --template minimal --packageManager pnpm --no-modules . When this guide was prepared that command produced the exact project described below. Now move into the folder and start the development server: Checkpoint Open http://localhost:3000 . You should see the Nuxt welcome page. In the terminal you will see a line saying the server is listening on that address. Press Ctrl+C in the terminal to stop the server whenever you want. What the starter gave you # The minimal Nuxt 4 starter contains these files (we checked by generating one): In programming terms Nuxt 4 uses an app/ folder for everything the browser runs (pages, components, composables). Server code lives in server/ at the project root, next to it. Nuxt 3 projects kept both at the root; this guide uses the Nuxt 4 layout. Nuxt also generates a hidden .nuxt/ folder with types; you never edit it, and .gitignore keeps it out of git. The starter's package.json scripts are what you will use all the time: pnpm dev : run the server with live reload while you work. pnpm build / pnpm preview : make and test a production build. Save your first commit # In plain words A commit is a save point you can always return to. Make one at the end of every chapter. If something breaks later, you can compare against your last good save."},{"t":"Packages, settings and secrets","u":"02-config.html","x":"The starter is empty. Now we add the libraries Clautverse uses and set up the configuration files. Nothing visible changes yet; this is the groundwork. On Windows: step by step # Everything on this page works on Windows. A few commands need a different spelling, and this section covers all of them. If you would rather skip the differences, use Git Bash (installed with Git for Windows) or WSL (Ubuntu inside Windows). Both run the guide's commands exactly as written. The steps below use PowerShell , which is already on your computer. Open PowerShell. Press the Windows key, type PowerShell , press Enter. Check the tools from chapter 1. Run node -v (it must print 24 or higher), git --version and pnpm -v . Anything missing can be installed from PowerShell with the Windows package manager: Close and reopen PowerShell after installing so it sees the new programs. Allow pnpm to run. If PowerShell says running scripts is disabled on this system when you type pnpm , run this once, then reopen PowerShell: Work in a short, plain folder. Keep the project somewhere like C:\\dev\\clautverse . Avoid folders synced by OneDrive or very long paths; syncing and long paths are common sources of odd install errors on Windows. Install the libraries. Inside the project folder, run the two pnpm add commands from the next section exactly as shown. They are single lines, so they work in PowerShell unchanged. Check that it worked. You should see every package from the table below with a version number. Then run pnpm dev and open http://localhost:3000 . In plain words None of the project's packages should need a C++ compiler. If pnpm ever prints a failed \"build\" step for a package, copy the first error line and look it up; the most common cause on Windows is a project folder inside OneDrive or a path that is too long. In programming terms winget is Windows' built-in package manager (Windows 10 and 11). pnpm-workspace.yaml (later on this page) tells pnpm which packages may run install scripts; that file is the same on every system. Windows differences used later in the guide Random secrets: openssl is not installed on Windows. Use Node instead (the \"Secrets\" section below shows it). Loading .env : set -a; . ./.env; set +a (chapter 5) is bash only. The \"Secrets\" section gives the PowerShell version. curl : in Windows PowerShell, curl is a different tool. Type curl.exe , and note that the long curl examples with 'single quoted JSON' are far easier in Git Bash or WSL. The API Explorer page (chapter 19) is a friendlier way to send those requests. Database commands: see PostgreSQL without Docker if you do not want to use Docker Desktop (which needs WSL 2 on Windows). Install the libraries # In plain words A package is code someone else wrote and shared. Instead of writing our own login system or database layer we install tested ones. pnpm add downloads a package and records it in package.json . Run these two commands inside the clautverse folder. The versions are the ones this guide was written with. Using the same ones avoids surprises. Package What we use it for Chapter better-auth Accounts, sign-in, sessions, email verification 8 drizzle-orm Describe tables and run queries in TypeScript 4, 5 postgres The driver: the actual connection to PostgreSQL 5 zod Checking the shape of incoming data 7 yaml Reads the API contract file for the Explorer 18 drizzle-kit (dev) Turns table definitions into migration files 4, 5 vitest , @nuxt/test-utils (dev) Run the automated tests, including starting the app for end-to-end tests 20 typescript , vue-tsc , @types/node (dev) Type checking for the server and the Vue page 21 In programming terms dependencies are needed when the app runs; devDependencies only while building and testing. postgres is pinned to an exact version (no ^ ); the others allow compatible updates. Add the scripts # Open package.json . The starter already has dev , build , generate , preview and postinstall . Add the rest so the scripts section matches this, an"},{"t":"Your first endpoint","u":"03-first-endpoint.html","x":"Time to make the server answer something. You will write a tiny throwaway endpoint, call it, and learn the three rules that explain all ~100 real endpoints in this project. A throwaway endpoint # Create the folder server/api and inside it a file called hello.get.ts : With pnpm dev running, open a second terminal and ask for it: Checkpoint You should get {\"message\":\"Hello from Clautverse\"} back. You can also paste http://localhost:3000/api/hello into your browser's address bar. You just built an API endpoint. In plain words curl is a command-line \"mini browser\". It sends a request and prints the answer. We use it so you can see exactly what the server says, without any page design in the way. Rule 1: the file name is the address # Nuxt's server engine is called Nitro . It looks at every file under server/api and turns its path into a web address, with no routing code to write. The .get part of the file name says which HTTP method it answers. File (in server/api ) Answers health.get.ts GET /api/health communities/index.get.ts GET /api/communities ( index means \"the folder itself\") communities/[id]/join.post.ts POST /api/communities/ anything /join . [id] is a parameter : whatever is in that spot becomes id . drops/[id]/reactions/me.put.ts PUT /api/drops/ anything /reactions/me [...].ts Any /api/... address nothing else matched (a \"catch-all\"). We use it in chapter 6 for tidy \"not found\" answers. auth/[...all].ts Everything under /api/auth/ ; handed to the login library (chapter 8) In programming terms This is file-based routing . Several files can share a folder with different methods: index.get.ts and index.post.ts in the same folder are two endpoints on one path. A parameter is read inside the handler with getRouterParam(event, 'id') (the project wraps this in a helper in chapter 7). Try a parameter. Create server/api/echo/[word].get.ts : Checkpoint The answer is {\"youSaid\":\"banana\"} . Rule 2: return an object and it becomes JSON # A handler is a function. Whatever it returns is sent back; objects and arrays are turned into JSON automatically. To change the status number you call setResponseStatus(event, 201) , and to refuse a request you throw an error. You will see all three in the real files. In plain words A handler is like a shop assistant: the request arrives ( event is the customer's order slip), the assistant does the work, and hands back an answer. Rule 3: some functions you never have to import # Notice that defineEventHandler and getRouterParam were used without any import line. Nitro auto-imports its built-in helpers. It does the same for every function we export from files in server/utils . That is why the real endpoints you will read later call requireUser , useDb or readJsonBody without importing them. In programming terms Auto-imports are a build-time feature: Nitro scans server/utils and generates the imports for you. The trade-off is that you cannot \"click through\" from an import line, so this guide always says which file a helper lives in. It also means two utils must not export the same name. Clean up # These two files were only for learning. Delete them so they do not clutter the project: Checkpoint curl http://localhost:3000/api/hello no longer returns the greeting (the server now answers \"not found\"). That is expected, and there is nothing to commit from this chapter."},{"t":"Run PostgreSQL and design the tables","u":"04-database-tables.html","x":"Almost everything in Clautverse is remembered: people, communities, posts, points. In this chapter you start a database and describe its tables. The next chapter connects the app to it. Start PostgreSQL with Docker # Tip No Docker? You can install PostgreSQL directly instead. See PostgreSQL without Docker for installs and drop-in replacements for every Docker command. The guide uses PostgreSQL 17 . In plain words A database is a program that stores data safely and answers questions about it quickly. Docker lets you run that program in an isolated \"container\" so you do not have to install and configure it yourself. If you delete the container you delete the database, which is perfect for learning. With Docker Desktop open, run this one command (it is the same one the project's README uses): -d : run in the background. --name clautverse-pg : a name so you can find it later. -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -e POSTGRES_DB=clautverse : set the password to postgres and create a database called clautverse . (Fine for a throwaway local database; never use this password anywhere real.) -p 5432:5432 : make the database reachable at port 5432 on your computer. postgres:17 : the program and version to run. Checkpoint docker ps lists clautverse-pg with status \"Up\". If you restart your computer, start it again with docker start clautverse-pg . Your .env already points at it. The line is NUXT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/clautverse , which reads as postgres:// user : password @ host : port / database . What an ORM does # In plain words A table has columns (like a spreadsheet's headings) and rows (the entries). Drizzle lets us describe each table in TypeScript, once. From that one description we get the real table, and also a typed way to ask for rows, so the editor warns us if we mistype a column name. In programming terms Drizzle is a \"schema-as-code\" ORM. A table is a pgTable('name', { columns }) call. Its helpers $inferSelect and $inferInsert give you the TypeScript type of a row you read or write. Column names in the database are snake_case ( created_at ); the TypeScript keys are camelCase ( createdAt ). The Drizzle settings file # Create drizzle.config.ts in the project root. It tells drizzle-kit where the table descriptions are and where to write migration files: Tables from the login library # Better Auth (chapter 8) needs five tables of its own: user , session , account , verification and rate_limit . We define them ourselves so we also control extra columns on user (a username, a date of birth, roles). Create server/db/schema/auth.ts : Things worth noticing: user.username , dateOfBirth , roles , status , onboardingStatus are Clautverse's own columns. roles is a comma-separated text such as fan,talent . status can only be active , suspended or pending_deletion . The { enum: [...] } option gives TypeScript that restriction. references(() = user.id, { onDelete: 'cascade' }) means \"this column points at a user; if the user is deleted, delete this row too\". That is how a session disappears with its user. timestamp(..., { withTimezone: true }) stores moments in time with their time zone, which avoids a whole class of date bugs. Tables for the app itself # Here is every table in server/db/schema/app.ts at a glance. You do not need to memorise it; later chapters introduce each table right before the code that uses it. Table Holds Chapter api_rate_limit Counters for \"too many requests\" limits 7 category , country Fandom categories; countries 10 talent The Stars 10 onboarding A new fan's picks (category, favourite Stars) 10 community , membership A Star's community; who joined it, their lane and Originals number 11 drop , drop_reaction , reply , idempotency_key Posts, likes, replies, and a table that makes \"send twice\" safe 12 mission , mission_completion , xp_transaction , app_setting Missions, who completed them, the XP ledger, admin-set rules 13 badge , badge_award Badges and who earned them 13 watch_party , watch"},{"t":"Connect, migrate, seed and check health","u":"05-connect-migrate.html","x":"Now the app meets the database. You will write the connection helper, create the real tables, add a few starter rows, and build the first real endpoint: a health check. The connection helper # Create the folder server/utils and the file server/utils/db.ts : In plain words Opening a connection is slow, so we open it once, the first time anyone needs it, and then reuse it. useDb() is the door every endpoint walks through to reach the database. In programming terms let _db at the top is a lazy singleton : created on first call, reused afterwards. useRuntimeConfig().databaseUrl reads the setting from chapter 2. No URL means a clear error instead of a confusing one later. prepare: false turns off prepared statements. The comment explains why: it keeps us compatible with Neon's pooled (PgBouncer) database endpoint, a hosted Postgres we may use later. max: 5 allows at most five connections at once. export { schema } makes the table definitions available everywhere. Because Nitro auto-imports exports from server/utils , any endpoint can write useDb() and schema.community with no import. Create the real tables (migrations) # In plain words A migration is a dated instruction like \"add this drawer to the filing cabinet\". Instead of editing the database by hand, we generate instructions from our TypeScript and run them in order. Anyone who runs the same instructions ends up with the same cabinet, and every change is saved in git. First, make your .env values visible to the two Drizzle commands. (Nuxt reads .env by itself when the app runs, but drizzle-kit is a separate tool and does not.) Run this once per terminal window: Windows PowerShell: use the .env loader from chapter 2 instead of the line above. Then generate and apply the migration: db:generate compares your schema files with the previous state and writes a numbered SQL file into server/db/migrations , such as 0000_something_random.sql , plus a meta folder that remembers the state. The random name part is chosen by the tool. db:migrate runs any migration files the database has not seen yet. In programming terms Open the generated .sql file: it is ordinary CREATE TABLE statements, one per table, with the constraints from your TypeScript. The project you are copying has nine migration files because its tables grew over time (for example shows and seasons were added and later removed). Yours will have one, because you are creating the final shape in one go. The result is the same set of tables. Checkpoint (Local PostgreSQL without Docker? Use the psql \"$NUXT_DATABASE_URL\" form from the appendix instead of docker exec .) Look inside the database. This runs a command inside the Docker container and lists the tables: You should see 30 tables. (Drizzle also keeps its own record of which migrations have run, in a separate place that this list does not show.) Watch out After this point, never change a table by editing the database by hand . Edit the schema file, run pnpm db:generate , then pnpm db:migrate . That keeps your project and your database in agreement. Starter data (the seed) # The tables are empty. Fans pick a category and favourite Stars during onboarding, so we need some to exist. Create scripts/seed.mjs : It inserts three categories (only Reality TV is active ), three countries, and three placeholder Stars (Ada, Bisi, Chidi). on conflict (slug) do nothing makes the script idempotent : running it twice does not create duplicates. sql.begin(async (tx) = ...) wraps everything in a transaction, so either all rows go in or none. Checkpoint The terminal prints seeded . The health endpoint # A health check is a tiny endpoint that says \"I am running, and my database answers\". Hosting services and humans ping it. Create server/api/health.get.ts : setResponseHeader(event, 'Cache-Control', 'no-store') tells browsers and proxies not to remember the answer. select 1 is the cheapest possible question to the database. If it answers, the database is alive. On failure the endpoint still repl"},{"t":"One shape for every error","u":"06-errors.html","x":"Things go wrong: a missing page, a forbidden action, a bug. A good API reports each of them in one predictable shape so the app using it can react properly. This chapter builds that. Why a standard error shape # In plain words Imagine every shop in a mall using a different kind of receipt. Cashiers would waste time decoding them. We make our server print the same receipt for every problem. Every error from Clautverse looks like this: code : a fixed word a program can check ( NOT_FOUND , FORBIDDEN , VALIDATION_FAILED ...). message : a sentence for a human. requestId : a label you can quote when asking \"what happened to request X?\". details : optional extras, such as which input field was wrong. Step 1: the list of error codes # Create server/utils/api-error.ts : ERROR_STATUS pairs each code with its HTTP status number. 401 UNAUTHENTICATED means \"I do not know who you are\"; 403 FORBIDDEN means \"I know who you are, and no\". 409 means the request clashes with the current state. as const freezes the list so TypeScript knows the exact words. ErrorCode = keyof typeof ERROR_STATUS then means \"one of these words\", so a typo like 'NOTFOUND' is caught while you type. apiError(code, message, details?) builds an error object. You use it as throw apiError('NOT_FOUND', 'Community not found.') . Step 2: stamp every request with an ID # Middleware is code that runs on every request before the endpoint. Create server/middleware/00.request-id.ts : It makes an ID like req_ plus a random UUID, stores it on event.context (a notebook that lives for one request) and also sends it back in an X-Request-Id header. The 00. in the file name controls order: middleware files run alphabetically, so this one runs first. Step 3: the one error handler # Create server/error-handler.ts . Every error thrown anywhere in the server ends up here: It works out the code: our own apiError already carries one in data.code ; for other errors it guesses from the status number (404 becomes NOT_FOUND , 401 UNAUTHENTICATED , and so on). Server bugs never leak. For any 5xx status the code becomes INTERNAL , the message becomes the harmless \"Something went wrong.\", and the real error goes only to the terminal log, tagged with the request ID. details are included only for 4xx errors. Security note Showing a raw error message or stack trace to the outside world can reveal file paths, database names and other secrets. That is why 5xx messages are replaced. Now tell Nitro to use this file. In nuxt.config.ts add this block after runtimeConfig : In programming terms ~~ means \"the project root\" (a single ~ means the app/ folder in Nuxt 4). Restart pnpm dev after changing nuxt.config.ts . Step 4: a catch-all for unknown addresses # Without this, a typo such as /api/nope would return Nuxt's HTML \"page not found\". An API should answer in JSON. Create server/api/[...].ts : Checkpoint With the app running: You should see status 404 , an x-request-id: req_… header, and a JSON body whose error.code is NOT_FOUND and whose error.requestId equals the header. (The project's own test checks exactly this, in chapter 20.) Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"Standard error shape and request IDs\" ."},{"t":"Checking input, paging and rate limits","u":"07-input.html","x":"Three helpers that almost every endpoint uses: one that checks incoming data, one that splits long lists into pages, and one that limits how often someone can call. We also write our first automated test. Checking incoming data with Zod # In plain words Never trust what arrives from outside. A request might have a missing field, a number where text should be, or something hostile. So each endpoint first describes the shape it expects, and anything that does not fit is rejected with a clear message before it can touch the database. Zod lets us describe that shape in code. A tiny example, which is not part of the project: This says: the body must be an object with a lane that is one of two words, optionally an alertsEnabled true/false, and nothing else ( .strict() rejects unknown fields; most real endpoints use it, so a typo in a field name is an error rather than silently ignored). Create server/utils/validate.ts , the helpers every endpoint uses to apply such a shape: toFields turns Zod's list of problems into { \"lane\": \"Invalid option…\" } , which is what the error's details.fields shows. Array positions are written like links[0].url . readJsonBody(event, schema) reads the request body, refuses invalid JSON, then validates it. readQuery does the same for ?a=1 b=2 parts of the address. readIdParam(event) reads [id] from the path and insists it is a UUID. A malformed ID is answered with NOT_FOUND (not a validation error), so a made-up ID looks the same as an ID that simply does not exist. readUserIdParam exists because user IDs from Better Auth are opaque text, not UUIDs. In programming terms Every failure is thrown as apiError('VALIDATION_FAILED', …) from chapter 6, so a bad request automatically gets the standard error shape. z.infer T gives you the TypeScript type of validated data, so after readJsonBody returns you are working with correctly typed values. Long lists: pages and cursors # In plain words A list of 50,000 communities cannot be sent in one go. We send a page (say 20), plus a bookmark (\"cursor\") saying where the next page starts. The caller sends the bookmark back to get the next page. In programming terms The classic way is page=2 (offset pagination), but it breaks when rows are added while someone is scrolling: items shift and you see duplicates or skip some. We use keyset pagination : the bookmark records the last item's sort values (such as its timestamp and ID), and the next query says \"give me rows after this one\". That stays stable and is fast with an index. The bookmark must be tamper-proof ; otherwise someone could invent one. So it is signed. Create server/utils/cursor.ts : The cursor is base64url(json) + \".\" + base64url(signature) . The signature is an HMAC (a fingerprint made with a secret, here cursorSecret from chapter 2). When decoding, we recompute the fingerprint and compare with timingSafeEqual , which takes the same time whether or not the first bytes match. That prevents guessing a signature by timing. A forged or garbled cursor decodes to null . Now the helper that endpoints use. Create server/utils/paginate.ts : pageQuery is the shared ?limit= cursor= shape: limit from 1 to 100 (default 20). z.coerce.number() turns the text \"20\" from the address into a number. readCursor(cursor, scope) verifies the signature and that the cursor was made for the same scope . The scope is a label such as com-popular ; it stops a cursor from the \"popular\" list being reused on the \"newest\" list. toPage(rows, limit, …) expects you to ask the database for limit + 1 rows. If you got more than limit , there is a next page, and the last kept row becomes the new cursor. Fetching one extra row avoids a separate \"is there more?\" query. The result is always { items, nextCursor } ; nextCursor is null on the last page. Rate limiting # In plain words Without limits, one person (or a script) could hammer the server or try thousands of passwords. A rate limit says \"at most N calls per minute\". Create server/utils/rate-limit.ts"},{"t":"Accounts, sign-in and sessions","u":"08-accounts.html","x":"Almost everything interesting in Clautverse needs to know who is asking. This chapter adds sign-up, sign-in, email verification and a one-line guard, requireUser , that private endpoints call first. The ideas # In plain words Authentication answers \"who are you?\" (you sign in). Authorization answers \"are you allowed to do this?\" (a fan may join a community; only the Star may post in it). After you sign in, the server gives your browser a session cookie , like a festival wristband. On later requests the browser shows it automatically, so you do not log in again for every click. In programming terms Passwords are never stored; Better Auth stores a salted hash in account.password . The session token lives in the session table and is sent back as a cookie ( better-auth.session_token ). We do not write any of the cryptography ourselves, which is the right call for security-sensitive code. Security Authentication code is where mistakes hurt most. Read this chapter to understand it, but before putting real users on any system like this, have someone experienced review it. Small helpers first: age and email # Clautverse is 18+. Create server/utils/age.ts : ageOn(dateOfBirth, today) counts whole years. It subtracts one if this year's birthday has not happened yet. The today parameter has a default ( new Date() ) so tests can pass a fixed date. isValidIsoDate accepts only real dates in YYYY-MM-DD form. Note how it builds the date and checks it prints back the same: that catches 2001-02-29 , which JavaScript would otherwise quietly turn into 1 March. And its test, tests/unit/age.test.ts : Sign-up and password-reset emails go through one function. Create server/utils/email.ts : In plain words While you develop, no real emails are sent: the text is printed in your terminal, including the confirmation link you would normally click in your inbox. If you later add a Mailtrap account, put its token in .env and the same code sends real mail. In programming terms $fetch is Nitro's built-in HTTP client. In production a missing token throws, so you can never silently send nothing. process.env.NODE_ENV is production only in a real deployment. The Better Auth setup # Create server/utils/auth.ts . It is the biggest file so far; the walk-through below explains each block. Block by block Constants at the top. USERNAME_RE allows 3 to 30 characters of a-z , 0-9 and _ . RESERVED_USERNAMES blocks names like admin and support . DELETION_GRACE_DAYS = 30 is the \"change your mind\" window for account deletion (chapter 9). emailAndPassword : password login is on, an unverified email cannot sign in ( requireEmailVerification ), passwords are 8 to 128 characters, and resetting a password signs out other devices. sendResetPassword calls our sendEmail . emailVerification : a confirmation email is sent at sign-up, and clicking the link signs the person in. user.additionalFields : tells Better Auth about our extra columns. input: true means a sign-up request may supply it; input: false means only the server may set it, so nobody can sign up with roles: \"super_admin\" . returned: false hides a field from responses (date of birth stays private). rateLimit : Better Auth's own limiter, stored in the rate_limit table, 30 calls per 60 seconds, switchable with the authRateLimitEnabled setting. databaseHooks.user.create.before : runs just before a new user row is saved. It lower-cases and checks the username, checks the date format, enforces the minimum age ( UNDER_MINIMUM_AGE ), checks the username is not taken, and forces safe defaults ( roles: 'fan' , status: 'active' ) no matter what the caller sent. The comment explains a real bug the project hit: querying through a second database connection while Better Auth holds a transaction can freeze the connection pool when many sign-ups arrive together, so the hook uses Better Auth's current transaction ( getCurrentAdapter ). databaseHooks.session.create.before : runs before every sign-in. A suspended user is refused ( ACCOUNT_SU"},{"t":"My account: profile and deletion","u":"09-profile.html","x":"Now that people can sign in, they need to see and change their own details. These three endpoints all live under /api/me : \"me\" means \"whoever the session cookie says is calling\". Read my profile # Create server/api/me/index.get.ts : In plain words One line does the whole job: requireUser finds out who is calling, and toMe shapes the answer. The { verified: false } option lets a person who has not yet confirmed their email still call this, so an app can show \"check your inbox\". In programming terms Any endpoint file is just export default defineEventHandler(async (event) = { … }) . The value returned from the function becomes the JSON response, so toMe(...) being the last expression is the response. Change my username or display name # Create server/api/me/index.patch.ts : PATCH means \"change some fields\". The Zod shape has .strict() (no unknown fields) and a .refine(...) requiring at least one field. The username must match the same rule used at sign-up ( USERNAME_RE from chapter 8) and not be reserved. Once every 30 days: if usernameChangedAt is recent the answer is INVALID_STATE with the date when the next change is allowed. Taken usernames: we check first, but two people could still race for the same name at the same instant. So we also catch the database's own uniqueness error ( code === '23505' ) and turn it into the same friendly CONFLICT . Checking and relying on the database's rule is the safe pattern. Delete my account (with a grace period) # Deleting someone's data is serious, so it is a two-step process: the request marks the account for deletion, and a daily background job (chapter 14) erases it 30 days later. Signing in during those 30 days cancels it (that hook was in auth.ts ). Create server/api/me/index.delete.ts : The caller must send their password again, so a stolen session alone cannot delete the account. It is checked with Better Auth's own verifier ( ctx.password.verify ), never by us. Rate limited to 5 attempts per hour, because this endpoint accepts password guesses. One transaction sets status = 'pending_deletion' and deletes all of the user's sessions (signing them out everywhere). A transaction means either both happen or neither. It clears the cookie and answers 202 Accepted (\"we will do it later\") with the date the purge will happen. Checkpoint With your jar.txt from chapter 8 (sign in again if you deleted it): Expect: your profile; the profile with the new display name; a VALIDATION_FAILED error (its details point at the unrecognised key); and finally UNAUTHENTICATED because the last call sent no cookie. Do not try the delete call on the account you want to keep. Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"My account endpoints\" . In programming terms You have now seen the usual order inside a write endpoint: 1) authenticate, 2) read and validate input, 3) rate limit if abuse is plausible, 4) check business rules, 5) change the database inside a transaction, 6) return a shaped response. Most of the next chapters repeat this order."},{"t":"Categories, Stars and onboarding","u":"10-catalog.html","x":"A new fan's first job is to choose what they are into. This chapter adds the public lists (categories, countries, Stars) and the three onboarding endpoints that save the fan's choices as they go. Shared helpers # Create server/utils/catalog.ts : list(items) wraps a short list in the same { items, nextCursor } shape as paged lists, with nextCursor: null . It keeps all list answers looking alike. validateSelections checks that the picked category exists and is active , and that every picked Star exists and belongs to that category. If anything is wrong it throws one VALIDATION_FAILED whose details.fields names each problem, such as talentIds[1]: not_in_category . Create server/utils/onboarding.ts : In plain words Onboarding is a short checklist: pick a category, pick favourite Stars, review. nextStep tells the app which step to show next, so a fan who closes the app halfway resumes where they stopped. Public lists # These three endpoints need no sign-in. Create server/api/categories.get.ts : server/api/countries.get.ts : And the Stars. server/api/talents/index.get.ts lists or searches them: Optional filters: categoryId and a search text q . ilike is a case-insensitive \"contains\" search. The text is first cleaned with q.replace(/[%_\\\\]/g, '\\\\$ ') because % and _ are wildcards in SQL; without this a visitor could type % and match everything. Results are capped at 200 so one call can never return an unbounded list. server/api/talents/[id]/index.get.ts shows one Star with their country: The onboarding endpoints # server/api/onboarding/index.get.ts returns where the fan is: server/api/onboarding/index.put.ts saves partial progress: PUT here means \"store this version of my picks\". It merges what arrived with what was saved before, so sending only categoryId keeps earlier Star picks. insert(...).onConflictDoUpdate(...) is an upsert : create the row if it does not exist, otherwise update it. The onboarding table has one row per user, so this is safe to repeat. server/api/onboarding/complete.post.ts finishes it: Needs a category and at least one Star ( .min(1, 'pick at least one Star') ). Marks the user's onboardingStatus as completed and saves the picks in one transaction . From now on, requireUser(event, { onboarded: true }) (chapter 8) lets this fan into endpoints such as joining a community. Others answer ONBOARDING_REQUIRED . Two helper files that later chapters rely on # Several endpoints you will write soon (community edits, drops, discover) call small helpers that belong to the \"Star\" side of the product. We create them now so nothing is missing later. Chapter 15 explains them in context. Create server/utils/talent-applications.ts : And server/utils/talentProfile.ts : For now, note two things: rejectMediaIds answers FEATURE_DISABLED whenever someone sends an uploaded-file ID (uploads are not built yet), and talentView / talentCard shape a Star for the API. Checkpoint The public lists first (no cookie needed): You should see Reality TV with \"active\":true and the three seeded Stars Ada, Bisi and Chidi. Copy the Reality TV id and the Star ids. Then, signed in ( jar.txt ), walk through onboarding; replace the placeholders: The first call shows \"status\":\"not_started\" and \"nextStep\":\"category\" . After PUT the next step becomes stars . After complete the status is completed and nextStep is null . Try completing with a made-up Star id to see the per-field error details. Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"Catalog and onboarding\" ."},{"t":"Communities, joining and Originals","u":"11-communities.html","x":"A community is the home of one Star's fans. This is the heart of Clautverse, and it contains the trickiest rule: the first 100 members get permanent \"Originals\" numbers, even when hundreds of people join at the same instant. The shared toolbox: admin.ts # Before communities, we add the helper file that many chapters use. Despite its name it holds more than admin code. Create server/utils/admin.ts : Helper What it does Tx , DbOrTx Types for \"a database transaction\" and \"either the database or a transaction\", so a helper can work inside or outside one. requireAdmin(event) requireUser plus a check for the super_admin role. Non-admins get FORBIDDEN . audit(tx, actorId, action, …) Writes one row to the append-only admin_audit table: who did what to what. notify(tx, userId, type, data) Adds an in-app notification (chapter 14). grantRole Adds a role to the comma-separated list without duplicating it. slugify , uniqueSlug Turn \"Ada O.\" into ada-o and find a free variant ( ada-o-2 …). ensureTalentCommunity Creates a Star's one community if it does not exist yet. publicUser The safe subset of a user (id, username, display name, avatar) that may be shown to others. No email, no birth date. suspendUser Suspends an account and signs it out everywhere (used in chapters 16 and 17). In plain words A view function such as publicUser is a deliberate filter between the database row and the outside world. Rows contain private things; views decide what is allowed to leave the building. Almost every …View function in this project exists for that reason. Community helpers # Create server/utils/community.ts : optionalUser(event) : for pages anyone can see, but which look different when signed in. It returns the user or null and never throws. isAdmin , isCommunityOwner : the two \"who is staff here?\" checks. loadVisibleCommunity(id, viewer, tx, lock) : loads a community, or throws NOT_FOUND . A vetoed community (hidden by an admin) looks non-existent to everyone except admins. With lock = true it adds FOR UPDATE , explained below. communityCard , communityDetail , membershipView : the shapes returned to callers. Notice originalPosition is hidden (null) for a removed member. ORIGINALS_CAP = 100 : the one place the number lives. Reading communities # server/api/communities/index.get.ts lists communities, searchable and sortable: In programming terms This file shows keyset pagination (chapter 7) for three different sorts. For \"popular\" the cursor holds (memberCount, id) and the filter is the SQL row comparison (member_count, id) (a, b) . The scope string includes the sort name, so a popular-cursor cannot be used on the newest list. Note limit + 1 from chapter 7 and toPage at the end. server/api/communities/[id]/index.get.ts is a good example of a tiny public endpoint: server/api/communities/[id]/index.patch.ts lets the Star (or an admin) edit name, description and rules: The Zod shape has no status field on purpose, so this endpoint cannot hide a community. That is a separate admin action (chapter 17). imageMediaId is accepted but rejected with FEATURE_DISABLED through rejectMediaIds (a helper from chapter 10), because uploads are not built yet. When an admin (not the owner) edits, the change is recorded with audit . Joining: the hard part # In plain words Picture a signing-up desk giving out numbered badges 1 to 100. If two assistants each look at the list, both see \"next number: 57\", and both hand out 57, you have a problem. The fix is to let only one assistant work at the desk at a time. In the database that is a row lock . Create server/api/communities/[id]/join.post.ts : Read it in this order: Guards. requireUser(event, { onboarded: true }) (finish onboarding first), the ID, a rate limit (60 per hour), and a Zod body. A lane is required , one of six codes. Open a transaction and lock the community row with loadVisibleCommunity(id, null, tx, true) . Anyone else joining the same community waits here until this transaction finishes. Already an active me"},{"t":"Drops, replies, likes and the feed","u":"12-drops.html","x":"A drop is a post by a Star in their community. This chapter covers creating and scheduling drops, deciding who may read them, replies and likes, and the feed a fan sees. The rules in plain words # Only the community's Star (or an admin) can post. Today only text drops work; voice and image answer \"feature disabled\" until uploads exist. A drop has an audience : public (anyone), members (the default: people who joined) or originals_only (only members with an Originals number). A drop is a draft until published. It can be published now, or scheduled for later. It becomes visible when its publishAt time passes. No background job is needed: the query simply asks for \"published or scheduled and publish time is in the past\". Someone who may not read a drop still sees a locked card : who posted, the type, the time and a short teaser, but never the body. Fans can like a drop once and reply to it (one level of nesting). The drops helper file # Create server/utils/drops.ts : Helper Job isLive , liveFilter \"Is this drop visible yet?\" as a JavaScript check and as a SQL condition. canSeeDrop The audience rule above, in four lines. dropView Builds the answer. If canSee is false it sets locked: true and body: null , so the body cannot leak by accident. loadDrop Loads one drop plus its community and Star, applying every visibility rule (vetoed community, archived or removed drop, not yet live, blocked author) and returning canSee , isStaff and the viewer's membership. dropListViews Shapes a whole page of drops while loading the viewer's memberships and likes in two queries total , not two per drop (this avoids the \"N+1 query\" slowdown). notifyDropPublished One SQL statement that creates a notification for every active member with alerts on (the author excluded). blockedAuthorIds , notAuthoredByBlocked Hide content from people the viewer blocked (chapter 16). The comment explains a subtle bug avoided: SQL's NOT IN silently drops rows with a NULL author, so the code uses OR author IS NULL . idempotencyHeader , findIdempotent , saveIdempotent The \"send it twice safely\" machinery, explained next. replyView Shapes a reply; a deleted or removed reply becomes a placeholder with no body or author. Idempotency: making \"send twice\" safe # In plain words On a bad phone connection an app may send \"create post\" and never hear the answer, so it sends again. Without protection the Star ends up with two identical posts. The fix: the app attaches a unique label (an Idempotency-Key ) to the request. If the server sees the same label again, it returns the original result instead of creating a second one. In programming terms The idempotency_key table stores (userId, scope, key) → resourceId . On create we first call findIdempotent ; if there is a hit we return that resource. Otherwise we create the resource and saveIdempotent inside the same transaction, so the key and the resource are saved together or not at all. The header is optional. Creating, reading, editing, publishing, deleting # server/api/drops/index.post.ts creates a drop: Order inside: authenticate, read the key, validate the body, replay check, rate limit (30 per minute), business rules (text only, body required, publishAt must be in the future, not both publishAt and publishNow ), permission (owner or admin), then the transaction. Status comes out as published ( publishNow ), scheduled (a future publishAt ) or draft . Only a published drop triggers member notifications. server/api/drops/[id]/index.get.ts reads one (with the locked-card rule): server/api/drops/[id]/index.patch.ts edits. Drafts and scheduled drops are fully editable; once live, only the text and teaser can change: server/api/drops/[id]/publish.post.ts moves a draft to published or scheduled: server/api/drops/[id]/index.delete.ts removes a drop. The author's delete archives it; an admin's delete marks it removed and is written to the audit log. Repeating the call is harmless (always 204 No Content ): Likes # server/api/drops/[id]/rea"},{"t":"Missions, XP and badges","u":"13-missions.html","x":"Missions give fans something to do. A Star posts a task, fans complete it, and points (XP) are awarded. The interesting engineering is in how points are stored: as a ledger, never as a number that gets overwritten. The rules in plain words # A mission belongs to one community and is created by its Star (or an admin). It has a title, an XP reward, an optional deadline, and a completion policy : review is auto (instant) or owner (the Star approves), and proof is none , link or text . The XP reward must be inside a range set by admins (default 5 to 100; stored in the app_setting table). Each fan can complete a mission once , and only if they joined the community. Auto missions pay out immediately. Owner-reviewed missions wait as submitted until approved or rejected with a reason. In plain words Think of XP like a bank statement rather than a balance on a sticker. Each earning or correction is a new line. The balance is the sum of the lines. Nobody can quietly change last week's line, so there is always a trail. The missions helper file # Create server/utils/missions.ts : grantXp is the only way XP changes. It inserts a ledger row. The table has a unique rule on (userId, sourceType, sourceId) , so granting for the same completion twice hits onConflictDoNothing and returns the original row instead of paying twice. getXpRules reads the admin-configured range and falls back to DEFAULT_XP_RULES . isExpired / missionStatus : expiry is computed from the deadline, not stored, so nothing has to run at midnight to flip it. loadMission : pending and rejected missions are visible only to staff and the creator. loadCompletionForReview : locks the completion row and insists the caller is staff of that mission's community. badgePatch and badgeBody : Zod shapes for admin badge endpoints (chapter 17). z.url({ protocol: /^https$/ }) accepts only https links. Missions # server/api/missions/index.get.ts lists missions. Active ones are ordered by deadline (soonest first, no deadline last); the other statuses by newest: server/api/missions/index.post.ts creates one: Rejects a reward outside the admin range, a deadline in the past, and an owner-reviewed mission without any proof (there would be nothing to review). Supports the Idempotency-Key header from chapter 12. server/api/missions/[id]/index.get.ts : Completing a mission # server/api/missions/[id]/completions/index.post.ts is where a fan submits: In programming terms Everything happens in one transaction : check the mission is open, check the fan is an active member, return the existing completion if there is one, check the proof, insert the completion, and, for auto-review, grantXp then link the ledger row back with xpTransactionId . If any step throws, the whole thing rolls back, so you can never get a completion without its XP or XP without its completion. Staff see the queue of submissions and fans see their own: The Star (or admin) approves or rejects. Approve grants XP once and notifies the fan; reject needs a reason and grants nothing: Repeating an approve returns the same result (safe to retry). Rejecting an already-approved completion is refused ( INVALID_STATE ): corrections are made with an admin XP adjustment instead (chapter 17), never by editing history. Reading my XP # server/api/xp/me.get.ts adds up the ledger: server/api/xp/me/transactions.get.ts lists every ledger line: In programming terms sum(amount) with coalesce(..., 0) returns 0 for someone with no rows. filter (where amount 0) gives lifetime earnings separately from the balance, which can go down after a negative admin correction. Badges # Admins create and award badges (chapter 17). Fans can read the public catalog and their own collection: Checkpoint As the admin user from chapter 12, create an auto-approved mission, complete it, and look at your XP (use your real community id): Expected: the completion has status approved , and /api/xp/me shows \"balance\":10 . Send the completion call again: you get the same completion ba"},{"t":"Watch parties, notifications and background jobs","u":"14-parties-notifications.html","x":"This chapter adds events fans can attend together, the bell that tells them things happened, and two jobs a scheduler calls to keep time-based things correct (reminders and account clean-up). Watch parties # In plain words A watch party is an event listing: a title, a start time, a link to wherever people will actually watch (another website), and an \"I'm going\" button. Clautverse does not host the video; it only organises the gathering and counts who is coming. Create server/utils/watchParties.ts : isIanaTimeZone accepts names like Africa/Lagos by asking JavaScript's built-in Intl to try to use the name. Times are stored in UTC; the zone is kept only so apps can show the local time properly. partyCreateBody and partyPatchBody : the Zod shapes. The patch shape is the create fields made .partial() (all optional), so the two cannot drift apart. assertPartyTimes : a new start time must be in the future and an end must come after the start. loadParty : loads a party with its community and host; \"staff\" means admin, community owner or host. notifyRsvps : one SQL statement that notifies everyone who RSVP'd. The endpoints. List upcoming or past parties: Create one (the Star or an admin). Members with alerts on get a notification, and the idempotency key from chapter 12 is supported: Read, edit and cancel: \"I'm going\" on and off. The two files are nearly identical on purpose ( const going = true versus false ): In programming terms The party row is locked ( loadParty(..., true) ) so two simultaneous RSVPs cannot both read rsvpCount = 5 and both write 6. The count only changes when a row was really inserted or deleted ( changed.length ), making repeated calls harmless ( idempotent ). Wherever you see PUT or DELETE on a \"me\" address in this project, expect this style. Spot the duplication The two RSVP files repeat about 20 lines. A shared helper would be tidier, but the project keeps them separate. When you are learning, notice it and decide for yourself; \"three similar lines are better than a premature abstraction\" is a common rule of thumb. Notifications # Notifications are rows in the notification table: a type such as drop.published and a small JSON data bag. Create server/utils/notifications.ts : SUMMARY turns a machine type into a sentence for the bell. An unknown type falls back to \"Update\". TARGETS finds the first ID in data ( dropId , partyId , …) to build a target telling the app where to jump when tapped. Four small endpoints: Every query includes userId = me , so nobody can read or mark anyone else's notifications. Marking someone else's returns NOT_FOUND . coalesce(read_at, now()) in read.post.ts keeps the first read time if you mark it again. Background jobs # In plain words Some things must happen \"on their own\": reminding people an hour before a party, switching a party from \"scheduled\" to \"live\", and finally erasing accounts whose 30-day deletion grace period ended. A scheduler (a cron service, or a timer on your host) calls two special addresses on a timetable. Those addresses must not be public, so they require a secret. Create server/utils/cron.ts : The caller sends Authorization: Bearer cronSecret . Any mismatch, including no secret configured at all, answers 404 , not 403: an outsider cannot even tell the job exists. The comparison uses timingSafeEqual (the same technique as the cursor) after checking lengths match. The notification job (meant to run every 5 to 15 minutes): The daily clean-up job: In programming terms Both jobs are written to be idempotent : running one twice does no harm. The reminder query marks reminder_sent_at in the same statement that inserts notifications (a \"CTE\", the with due as (…) part), so a party is never reminded twice. The purge job deletes at most 100 accounts per run; foreign keys set author columns to null, so posts and the XP ledger survive without a person attached. Checkpoint As your admin user, create a party in a future time, RSVP, then cancel it to trigger a notification (us"},{"t":"Becoming a Star","u":"15-stars.html","x":"Communities exist because Stars exist. A fan applies; an admin reviews (chapter 17); an accepted Star gets the talent role, a profile and their own community. This chapter builds the applicant's and the Star's side. Helpers (created in chapter 10) # You created server/utils/talent-applications.ts in chapter 10. Here is what its parts are for (the code is on that chapter's page and in the reference ): httpsLink : a labelled link that must start with https . Links are only accepted as https so nobody can store a javascript: address that would run code when clicked. rejectMediaIds : file uploads are not built yet, so any attempt to send an uploaded-file ID answers FEATURE_DISABLED with a friendly message. This is a deliberate placeholder. applicationView vs adminApplicationView : the applicant never sees the admin's internalNote or who decided. The admin view adds them. Same data, two audiences. REAPPLY_DAYS = 7 : after a decline, reapplyAfter tells the applicant when they may try again. decisionBody : the Zod shape for accept/decline (chapter 17). The claimView functions belong to identity claims, below. server/utils/talentProfile.ts , also from chapter 10, holds talentView (a Star's full profile) and talentCard (the short version used by discover). Applying # server/api/talent-applications/index.post.ts submits an application: A Star cannot apply again; the category must exist and be active; you may have only one pending application; after a decline you must wait out reapplyAfter . The \"one pending application\" rule is enforced twice: by a check in code (for a friendly message) and by the partial unique index talent_application_one_pending_uq in the schema (so even two simultaneous requests cannot both succeed). The code handles that race by catching the empty returning() result. Rate limited to 5 per hour. Look at your applications and edit a pending one: The Star's own screens # Once accepted, a Star has a dashboard with headline numbers and can edit their profile. Both need the talent role. In programming terms The dashboard runs three counting queries at the same time with Promise.all instead of one after another, which makes the endpoint about as fast as its slowest query rather than the sum. The destructuring [[nm], [pend], [wp]] unpacks \"the first row of each result\". There is no ID in the address: PATCH /api/talent/profile always edits my own profile. A fan cannot edit someone else's by guessing an ID, because the query is where user_id = me . Identity claims (scheduled for removal) # Heads-up An identity claim lets someone say \"this existing Star profile is me\" and attach evidence links for an admin to verify. The project plan ( docs/plan.md , task 3) removes this feature because the design document has no separate claim flow. The code still exists at this snapshot, so it is shown here so the guide matches it. If you are building your own version, you can safely skip these two files and the identity_claim table. Checkpoint Submit an application as your test user (use the Reality TV category id from chapter 10): The first call returns status pending with HTTP 201. Send it again and you get CONFLICT (\"You already have a pending application\"). Try a link beginning http:// and see it rejected. In chapter 17 you will accept this application as an admin. Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"Star applications, dashboard and profile\" ."},{"t":"Reports and blocks","u":"16-safety.html","x":"Any community product needs a way to deal with bad behaviour. Fans can report something to the admins, or block a person so they stop seeing that person's content. This chapter builds both, plus the helper admins use to remove a member. Reports # In plain words A report is a flag on a drop, reply, user, community or mission with a reason (spam, harassment, hate, sexual, impersonation, other). It joins a queue that admins work through in chapter 17. The person reported is never told who reported them. Create server/utils/reports.ts : isUniqueViolation recognises the database's \"that already exists\" error code ( 23505 ), even when wrapped inside another error. assertReportTarget : you may only report something you are able to see. It reuses loadDrop , loadVisibleCommunity and loadMission , so a hidden or non-existent target simply answers NOT_FOUND . reportSubject : for admins, finds the author and community behind whatever was reported. removeMember : the removal rule. A member removed from a community has their Originals number retired (the row keeps it; the counter never hands it out again). The first removal blocks rejoining for 30 days ; a second removal is permanent. The member is notified, the action is audited, and the member count goes down, all inside the caller's transaction. server/api/reports/index.post.ts : One open report per reporter and target. A repeat returns the existing report instead of creating spam. The check runs before the rate limit, so repeating is free. The reported item must be visible to the reporter. If two identical reports race, the database's unique index rejects the second; the catch block then loads and returns the first. Handling the race this way is common: let the database be the final judge, and turn its error into a normal answer. Blocks # In plain words A block is private and one-directional: if I block you, I stop seeing your drops and replies. You are not told, and nothing else changes for you. It is about what I see. server/api/users/[id]/block.post.ts and block.delete.ts : You cannot block yourself ( VALIDATION_FAILED ), and the target must exist. Blocking twice returns the existing block; unblocking something that is not blocked still succeeds. Both are idempotent, so apps can retry freely. The blocking itself is only a row. The effect happens elsewhere: the feed, community timeline, single-drop and reply endpoints from chapter 12 all call blockedAuthorIds and notAuthoredByBlocked . That is why those helpers live in drops.ts . This is the first place user IDs are used as a path parameter, so it uses readUserIdParam from chapter 7 (Better Auth IDs are not UUIDs). Checkpoint As your test user, report the drop from chapter 12, repeat the report, and try to block yourself (use your own user id from /api/me ): Expected: the first call returns status open with HTTP 201; the second returns the same report with HTTP 200; the block call returns VALIDATION_FAILED with fields.id = \"self\" . Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"Reports and blocks\" ."},{"t":"The admin area and the audit trail","u":"17-admin.html","x":"Admins keep the platform healthy. Their endpoints all live under /api/admin and share one rule: every action is checked, reasoned and recorded . This chapter shows the pattern with the most important routes and lists the rest. How admin access works # In plain words An admin is a normal account with an extra role word, super_admin , in its roles text. There is deliberately no public way to get that role: sign-up forces roles to fan (chapter 8). You grant it by hand in the database, as you did in the chapter 12 check: Every admin endpoint begins with const admin = await requireAdmin(event) (from admin.ts , chapter 11), which answers UNAUTHENTICATED or FORBIDDEN as appropriate. In programming terms The same four-part shape repeats in almost every admin write: 1) requireAdmin ; 2) validate the body (a reason is usually required); 3) a transaction that locks the row ( .for('update') ), checks its current state, changes it; 4) audit(...) and often notify(...) inside that same transaction , so a change can never exist without its audit row. Accepting a Star's application # This is the endpoint that turns a fan into a Star. You wrote the application side in chapter 15. Inside one transaction it: locks the application; returns quietly if it is already accepted (safe to retry); refuses if it was declined; creates the talent profile with a unique slug (or reuses one); creates the Star's community with ensureTalentCommunity ; adds the talent role with grantRole ; marks the application accepted; writes the audit row; and notifies the applicant. If any step fails, none of it happens. Working the report queue # server/api/admin/reports/[id]/actions.post.ts applies one of five actions to a report and closes it: dismiss , remove_content , suspend_user , remove_member or veto_community . It first looks up what was reported with reportSubject (chapter 16), and rejects an action that makes no sense for that target (for example remove_content on a user report) with not_applicable . suspend_user and remove_member reuse suspendUser and removeMember so the rules are written once. Hiding a community (veto) # Veto only flips status to vetoed . Memberships and Originals numbers are untouched, so a restore brings everything back. The hiding itself is done by loadVisibleCommunity (chapter 11). Correcting XP without rewriting history # A correction is a new ledger row (positive or negative, never zero) with a required reason and the admin's ID. A random UUID is used as sourceId because each adjustment is its own event. It is also written to the audit log. The audit log # This is read-only: no route in the project updates or deletes admin_audit rows. Admins can filter by who, what kind of target and a time range. Suspending a user # The handler is three lines because the logic lives in suspendUser (chapter 11): admins cannot be suspended, and suspending deletes the person's sessions so they are signed out everywhere. Every admin route # The remaining files follow the same pattern. Each link opens the full code in the reference pages, ready to copy. Area Route files (under server/api/admin ) Overview dashboard.get.ts (six counts, fetched in parallel) Applications applications/index.get.ts , applications/[id]/index.get.ts , accept (above), applications/[id]/decline.post.ts (needs a reason for the applicant) Identity claims (to be removed) claims/index.get.ts , claims/[id]/index.get.ts , claims/[id]/verify.post.ts , claims/[id]/reject.post.ts Communities communities/index.get.ts (includes vetoed ones), communities/index.post.ts , veto (above), communities/[id]/restore.post.ts , communities/[id]/members/[userId]/reinstate.post.ts Users users/index.get.ts (search), suspend (above), users/[id]/reinstate.post.ts Reports reports/index.get.ts (oldest first), actions (above) XP rules and corrections xp-rules.get.ts , xp-rules.put.ts , xp-adjustments (above) Badges badges/index.get.ts , badges/index.post.ts , badges/[id]/index.get.ts , badges/[id]/index.patch.ts , ba"},{"t":"The API contract (OpenAPI)","u":"18-contract.html","x":"So far the only description of our API is the code. This chapter adds a second one that machines and humans can both read: an OpenAPI contract . Then we add the one endpoint that serves it to the API Explorer. What OpenAPI is # In plain words A restaurant has a menu so customers know what they can order without walking into the kitchen. OpenAPI is the menu for an API: for each address it lists the method, what you must send, and what the answers look like. Tools can read it to build documentation pages, test clients and, in our case, the API Explorer. In programming terms OpenAPI (version 3.1 here) is a YAML or JSON file. The project keeps it by hand at openapi/clautverse.yaml and treats it as the source of truth for the Explorer. It is about 3,400 lines for roughly 110 operations, which is why this guide teaches the structure instead of asking you to type all of it. Anatomy of the file # The top of the file names the API: Under paths: each address has one entry per method. This is the simplest one, the health check: operationId and x-route-id : the route's short name ( OPS-01 ). Names starting with x- are our own additions. The same IDs appear in comments at the top of the route files (such as // OPS-01 ). tags : the group in the Explorer's sidebar ( OPS , COM , ADM …). x-status : implemented , planned or deferred . The Explorer shows a coloured badge for anything not implemented. responses.default points at a shared error description defined once, below. An operation that takes input adds parameters (for the path, the query string, headers) and a requestBody . Here is the join operation from chapter 11: Notice how each field describes itself: type , format , allowed values ( enum ), limits ( minimum , maxLength ), and an example . The Explorer turns exactly these into dropdowns, number boxes with limits and sample data. That is why the contract matters to the page you build in chapter 19. Finally, shared pieces live under components , so they are written once: The Error schema there lists the same error codes as chapter 6, and Page describes the { items, nextCursor } shape from chapter 7. Create your own starter file # In plain words You do not have to write 110 entries to continue. Start with a few and add more whenever you like. Each new route needs an entry that follows the join example above. Create openapi/clautverse.yaml with the header above, the /api/health block under paths: , and the components: section. To get the complete contract, copy the file from the project repository; it is the exact one the Explorer in this guide shows. The route that serves the contract # The browser cannot read a YAML file from the project folder, so we add an endpoint that reads it, converts it to JSON and sends it. It also adds one extra feature: for every operation it works out which parts of the system the route uses and attaches them as x-services labels (the little chips on each Explorer card). Create server/api/_docs/openapi.get.ts : Caching by file time. The parsed contract is kept in memory and only rebuilt when the file's modification time changes, so edits show up without restarting. routeKey converts a route file name to a contract path ( [id] becomes {id} , index disappears), the reverse of chapter 3's rule. utilExports and servicesPerRoute read every util file, collect the names it exports, then search each route file's text for those names. A route that mentions requireUser gets the \"Session guard\" chip. It is a simple text scan, not magic: it can only be as accurate as the names are distinctive. If anything fails, it answers 500 OpenAPI spec unavailable . In programming terms The folder name _docs has no special meaning to Nitro; the leading underscore is just a naming habit. The address is /api/_docs/openapi . Checkpoint Restart pnpm dev and fetch it: You should see JSON starting with {\"openapi\":\"3.1.0\",\"info\":{\"title\":\"Clautverse API\"… and then the paths. Commit: git add -A && git commit -m \"OpenAPI contract and docs en"},{"t":"The API Explorer page (Vue)","u":"19-explorer.html","x":"Up to now you tested with curl . Here you build the page that does the same thing with buttons and forms: the API Explorer. It is the only screen in the project, and a good first look at Vue inside Nuxt 4. What it does # Shows every operation from the OpenAPI contract, grouped by tag, with a search box. Has a sign-in / sign-up strip at the top, so requests run as a real user through the same session cookie you used in curl . Each operation is a card. Open it to see parameters and body fields as the right kind of input (dropdown for fixed choices, a picker of real records for IDs, number boxes that enforce limits), press Send request and see the status, timing and JSON answer. Vue and Nuxt in five minutes # In plain words A web page is built from components : small reusable pieces (a button, a card, a top bar) that each own their appearance and behaviour. Vue is the library for writing components. Nuxt adds conventions so you write less setup. In programming terms A .vue file has up to three blocks: script setup lang=\"ts\" (logic), template (HTML with extras), and optional styles. Here styling lives in one shared CSS file. ref(x) makes a reactive value: when it changes, the page updates. You read and write it with .value in script, but just by name in the template. computed(() = …) is a value derived from others. reactive({}) is a reactive object. watch(source, fn) runs code when something changes. Template extras: v-if / v-else (show or hide), v-for (repeat), v-model (two-way link between an input and a value), :prop=\"value\" (bind an attribute to JavaScript), @click=\"fn\" (handle an event). Nuxt conventions used here: a file in app/pages/ is a route ( index.vue is / ); components in app/components/ are used without importing, and a subfolder becomes a prefix ( components/docs/AuthPanel.vue is DocsAuthPanel ); exported functions in app/composables/ are auto-imported; and Vue's ref , computed , watch , onMounted are auto-imported too. Step 1: composables (the logic) # A composable is a plain function holding logic that components share. The first one fetches and understands the contract. Create app/composables/useOpenApi.ts : The interface blocks describe the shapes we read from the contract, so the editor can autocomplete them. specOperations(spec) flattens the nested paths → methods into one simple list of operations, pulling out our x-route-id , x-status and x-services fields. describeSchema writes a short rule such as string · uuid or one of: a, b ; bodyFields flattens a request body into table rows; groupByTag builds the sidebar groups. useOpenApi() calls /api/_docs/openapi (chapter 18) with useAsyncData , which fetches on the server for the first paint and shares the result across components. The second composable fills dropdowns with real records , so you pick an existing community instead of pasting a made-up ID. Create app/composables/useLookup.ts : BY_FIELD maps a field name ( communityId ) to the list endpoint that holds valid values ( /api/communities ). BY_PATH does the same for a path parameter called plain id , by looking at which route it belongs to. Each list is fetched once and cached in a Map , so ten cards asking for communities cause one request. A failed request (for example, not signed in) gives an empty list. reloadLookups() refetches everything. The sign-in strip calls it after signing in or out, because some lists are private. import.meta.client guards the fetch so it only runs in the browser. Step 2: small components # One input that picks its own type from the field's rule. Create app/components/FieldInput.vue : defineProps declares what the parent passes in; defineModel makes v-model work on this component. The template is a chain of v-if / v-else-if : fixed choices → dropdown; list of IDs → multi-select; known record list → dropdown; number → number box clamped to its min and max; date-time → date picker; otherwise plain text. The computed({ get, set }) blocks convert between what the user types and w"},{"t":"Automated tests","u":"20-tests.html","x":"You have been checking the app with curl and by clicking. Tests do the same thing automatically, in seconds, every time, and tell you the moment a change breaks a rule. This chapter explains the two kinds in this project and how to run them. Why tests matter here # In plain words Clautverse has rules that are easy to break by accident: \"an Originals number is never reused\", \"a second removal is permanent\", \"a suspended user cannot sign in\". A test is a written promise that a rule still holds. When a promise fails, you find out immediately, not from an angry user. Unit tests check one small function in isolation ( ageOn , encodeCursor ). They are instant and need nothing else running. You wrote two in chapters 7 and 8. End-to-end (e2e) tests start the real Nuxt app, talk to it over HTTP like a client would, and look at the real database. They are slower but prove the whole chain works. The test runner settings # You created vitest.config.ts in chapter 7. Look at it again: projects defines the two groups, unit and e2e , so pnpm test:unit and pnpm test:e2e can run them separately. fileParallelism: false makes e2e test files run one after another, because they share one database. Long timeouts for the e2e group allow for creating and migrating a database before the tests start ( hookTimeout ). A fresh database for every run # Create tests/e2e/global-setup.ts . It runs once before any e2e test: It drops and recreates a database called clautverse_test , so every run starts clean and your real development data is never touched. It runs the same migrations ( drizzle-kit migrate ) and the same seed you ran by hand in chapter 5. Tip Using a local PostgreSQL instead of Docker? See the appendix for the one setting the tests may need. Watch out Docker and the clautverse-pg container from chapter 4 must be running. Tests connect to localhost:5432 with the postgres user. Helpers shared by every e2e file # Create tests/e2e/helpers.ts : testEnv : the settings given to the test copy of the app (database, secrets, and the login rate limit switched off so tests are not throttled). newUser() : makes a unique, valid sign-up body each time. verifiedSession() : signs up, flips email_verified directly in the database (so no email is needed), signs in and returns the cookie. You did the same by hand in chapter 8. api(path, options) : a small wrapper around HTTP calls that returns { status, headers, body } . catalog() : looks up the seeded Reality TV and Music categories and the star Ada, which many tests need. In programming terms setup({ server: true, … }) from @nuxt/test-utils/e2e builds and starts the app on a free port; the helpers' fetch and url then target it. This is why @nuxt/test-utils was installed in chapter 2. Reading a real test file # tests/e2e/api.test.ts checks the basics from chapters 3 to 10: health, the error shape, sign-up rules, /api/me , account deletion, and onboarding: describe('name', () = { … }) groups tests; it('does something', async () = { … }) is one test. The first lines call await setup(…) to start the app, and afterAll(() = sql.end()) closes the test database connection when finished. Each test follows arrange, act, assert : create a user (arrange), call an endpoint (act), expect(…) something about the answer (assert). Look for the test \"unknown routes use the error envelope\" : it proves the catch-all file from chapter 6 works, and that error.requestId matches the response header. The sign-up race that once froze the database pool has its own regression test, so it can never silently come back: The other end-to-end files cover one feature each. Their code is in the reference pages, ready to copy: File What it proves tests/e2e/communities.test.ts Joining, lanes, Originals numbers, leaving, removal rules, admin veto tests/e2e/drops.test.ts Audiences, locked cards, scheduling, likes, replies, idempotency tests/e2e/missions.test.ts Mission rules, XP ledger, badges tests/e2e/watch-parties.test.ts Parties, RSVPs, reminders, t"},{"t":"Check everything and what comes next","u":"21-finish.html","x":"You built the whole thing. This last chapter is a checklist to prove it all works together, a troubleshooting table, and a look at what the project plans next. Type check the whole project # TypeScript can check every file without running anything: It runs nuxt typecheck , which uses vue-tsc to check both server and page code against the files Nuxt generates. A clean run prints no errors. If it fails Read the first error only. Most later errors are fallout from it. The file name and line number are given; open that line and compare with the code in the chapter. The final checklist # pnpm dev starts without errors; /api/health says ok . /api/nope answers a JSON NOT_FOUND with a request ID. You can sign up, verify, sign in and call /api/me . Onboarding completes; you can join a community and get Originals number 1. An admin can publish a drop, you can like it and see it in the feed. A mission completion adds XP once, never twice. A reported drop appears in the admin report queue; an accepted application creates a community. The API Explorer at / lists the operations and sends requests as you. pnpm test:unit and pnpm test:e2e pass. .env is not in git ( git status never lists it). When something goes wrong # You see Likely cause Try NUXT_DATABASE_URL is not set .env missing or the dev server was started before you created it. Check .env has the line; restart pnpm dev . Health shows \"db\":\"error\" and status 503 The database container is stopped, or the URL is wrong. docker ps ; docker start clautverse-pg . relation \"user\" does not exist Migrations were not applied. set -a; . ./.env; set +a then pnpm db:migrate . pnpm db:migrate complains about the URL The shell did not load .env (drizzle-kit does not read it by itself). Run the set -a; . ./.env; set +a line in the same terminal window. Sign-up works but sign-in says email not verified (403) This is correct behaviour. Click the link printed in the server terminal, or run the SQL shortcut from chapter 8. ONBOARDING_REQUIRED when joining The fan has not completed onboarding. Finish chapter 10's complete call. FORBIDDEN on /api/admin/* The account is not an admin. Run the chapter 17 SQL to grant super_admin . Cursor errors ( Invalid cursor ) NUXT_CURSOR_SECRET changed, or the cursor came from a different list. Start again from page one. Changed nuxt.config.ts but nothing happened The dev server needs a restart for config changes. Stop and rerun pnpm dev . Port 5432 already in use Another PostgreSQL is running on your machine. Stop it, or map Docker to another port and update NUXT_DATABASE_URL . Explorer dropdowns are empty You are signed out (some lists are private), or the list is empty. Sign in at the top of the page; create data. What is deliberately missing # Uploads (images, voice): routes that need them answer FEATURE_DISABLED . Deployment : this guide runs everything on your machine. The project is designed for a hosted Postgres (it mentions Neon) and a scheduler to call the two job routes from chapter 14, but deployment steps are outside this guide. A real front end for fans: only the API Explorer exists. The project's roadmap # The project keeps its plan in docs/plan.md . At this snapshot the table of tasks lists these as still to do: small fixes (task 2c), removing identity claims (3), seeding fictional pilot data (5), a 64 KB request-size limit (6), following Rising Stars (7), XP per fandom with tiers (8), a second lane at the Supporter tier (9), and a small analytics event log (10). When one lands, rebuild this guide with node tutorial/build.mjs and re-read the affected chapters. Where to go from here # Build a feature of your own end to end: a table in the schema, a migration, an endpoint with Zod and requireUser , an entry in the contract, a test. That one exercise uses every chapter. Read docs/specs/ecosystem-prd.md in the repository to see the product the code is aiming at. Pick one skill to deepen: SQL and transactions, Vue components, or TypeScript types. This project "},{"t":"PostgreSQL without Docker","u":"90-postgres-without-docker.html","x":"The guide uses Docker so the database is one command. If you would rather install PostgreSQL directly on your computer (or already have it), this page gives you the replacements. Everything else in the guide stays the same. Which version # In plain words PostgreSQL is the program that stores all the data. Docker was only a convenient box to run it in. You can run the same program directly on your computer instead, and the app will not notice the difference. The guide uses PostgreSQL 17 (the Docker image is postgres:17 , from the project's README). Install 17 if you can. The project's tests drop a database with with (force) , which needs PostgreSQL 13 or newer, so any recent version should work, but 17 is the one the guide was written against. Check yours with: 1. Install and start it # In plain words Installing gives you the database program plus a background \"service\" that keeps it running, like a shop that stays open while you work. System Install Notes macOS (Homebrew) brew install postgresql@17 then brew services start postgresql@17 Homebrew does not put psql on your path for versioned installs. Add /opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@17/bin to your PATH (on Intel Macs the folder starts /usr/local/opt ). Your login is a database superuser with no password . Ubuntu / Debian sudo apt install postgresql The version depends on your release; check with psql --version . The service starts automatically. The superuser is the system user postgres , reached with sudo -u postgres psql . Windows The installer from postgresql.org/download/windows The installer asks for a password for the postgres user and a port (keep 5432). Use \"SQL Shell (psql)\" from the Start menu, or add the bin folder to your PATH . In programming terms These commands come from each system's usual package route and are not tested by this guide. If one differs on your machine, the PostgreSQL download page for your system is the authority. 2. Create the database and a login # In plain words The Docker command created a database called clautverse with the user postgres and the password postgres in one go. Locally you do the same two things by hand. Open psql as the superuser (macOS: psql postgres ; Ubuntu: sudo -u postgres psql ; Windows: SQL Shell) and run: Checkpoint Still inside psql , type \\l . The list of databases should include clautverse . The second line is optional on macOS Homebrew, where you can use your own login without a password. It is a throwaway local password; never use it anywhere real. Type \\q to leave. 3. Point the app at it # In .env , set NUXT_DATABASE_URL to match what you created. The pattern is postgres:// user : password @ host : port / database : Your setup NUXT_DATABASE_URL You ran step 2 exactly postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/clautverse (the same as the guide) macOS Homebrew, your own login postgres://YOUR_MAC_USERNAME@localhost:5432/clautverse A different password or port Put your values in the same pattern Checkpoint Run set -a; . ./.env; set +a then psql \"$NUXT_DATABASE_URL\" -c \"select 1\" . A table with a 1 means the address, login and database are right. If you changed .env while pnpm dev was running, restart it. 4. Replace the Docker commands # Whenever the guide shows a docker command, use the right-hand column. They all assume you ran set -a; . ./.env; set +a in that terminal window first, so $NUXT_DATABASE_URL is set. Where Guide says Use instead Chapter 1 Install Docker Desktop, docker -v Skip. Do steps 1 to 3 on this page. Chapter 4 docker run … postgres:17 Skip. Steps 1 and 2 above replace it. Chapter 4 docker ps shows \"Up\"; docker start clautverse-pg macOS: brew services list ; brew services start postgresql@17 . Ubuntu: sudo systemctl status postgresql . Windows: the \"postgresql\" service in Services. Chapter 5 docker exec clautverse-pg psql -U postgres -d clautverse -c '\\dt' psql \"$NUXT_DATABASE_URL\" -c '\\dt' Chapter 5 docker stop clautverse-pg / docker start clautverse-pg (the health-check test) The service stop and"},{"t":"Glossary","u":"99-glossary.html","x":"Use the search box above or scan the list. Terms are alphabetical. Term In plain words In programming terms API A menu of things another program can ask our server to do. A set of HTTP endpoints returning JSON. Audit log A diary of what admins did, which nobody may edit. The append-only admin_audit table written by audit() . Auto-import Functions you can use without writing an \"import\" line. Nitro and Nuxt generate imports for server/utils , app/composables and components. Block Hiding another user's content from yourself. A row in block ; feed queries exclude those authors. Catch-all route A file that answers every address nothing else matched. [...].ts in server/api . Community The home of one Star's fans. A community row owned by one talent . Component A reusable piece of a web page. A Vue .vue file. Composable A shared piece of page logic. A function in app/composables using Vue reactivity. Cookie A small note your browser keeps and shows the site each visit. The session token travels in a cookie header. Cron / scheduler An alarm clock that calls our job addresses on a timetable. An external timer calling /api/internal/jobs/* with a bearer secret. Cursor A bookmark saying where the next page of a list starts. A signed, opaque keyset pagination token. Drizzle The tool that lets us describe tables and ask for rows in TypeScript. A typed SQL ORM and query builder ( drizzle-orm , drizzle-kit ). Drop A post by a Star. A drop row with an audience and a status. End-to-end test A test that runs the whole app like a real user would. A Vitest test using @nuxt/test-utils against a real database. Endpoint / route One thing you can ask the server for. HTTP method plus path, backed by one file in server/api . Environment variable A setting kept outside the code. Read via process.env or useRuntimeConfig() . Idempotent Safe to do twice; the second time changes nothing. Repeating the request leaves the same state (e.g. PUT like, Idempotency-Key). JSON Data written as text in a tidy format. JavaScript Object Notation. Keyset pagination Paging by \"after this item\" rather than \"page number\". A WHERE (a, id) (…) filter plus ORDER BY and LIMIT . Lane One of six labels a fan picks when joining. A text column on membership validated by a Zod enum. Ledger A list of every change, never edited. An append-only table; balance is SUM(amount) . Middleware Code that runs before every request is handled. Files in server/middleware , run in filename order. Migration A saved instruction for changing the database layout. A generated SQL file applied in order by drizzle-kit migrate . Mission / XP A task, and the points it earns. mission , mission_completion and xp_transaction tables. Nitro Nuxt's server engine. Turns server/ files into routes and handles requests. Nuxt The framework that runs both our server and our one page. A Vue meta-framework (version 4 here). OpenAPI A written menu of an API that tools can read. A YAML spec ( openapi/clautverse.yaml ). Originals The first 100 members, with permanent numbers. originalPosition allocated under a row lock from a never-decreasing counter. pnpm The tool that downloads the libraries we use. A Node package manager. Rate limit A cap on how often someone can call. A fixed-window counter in api_rate_limit . Reactive Updates the page automatically when data changes. Vue ref , reactive , computed . Request ID A label for one request, to trace problems. req_… set by middleware and returned in X-Request-Id . Row lock \"Only one person edits this row at a time.\" SELECT … FOR UPDATE ( .for('update') ). Schema The description of the tables. Drizzle pgTable definitions in server/db/schema . Seed Starter data to fill empty tables. scripts/seed.mjs . Session Proof you signed in, remembered by the server. A row in session , referenced by a cookie. Star / talent A public figure with a community. A talent row (code uses \"talent\"). Transaction \"All of these steps, or none.\" A database transaction ( db.transaction(async tx = …) )."},{"t":"Config and setup files","u":"ref-config.html","x":"Config and setup files source code reference"},{"t":"Database files","u":"ref-database.html","x":"Database files source code reference"},{"t":"Server helpers","u":"ref-helpers.html","x":"Server helpers source code reference"},{"t":"API routes","u":"ref-routes.html","x":"API routes source code reference"},{"t":"API Explorer (front end)","u":"ref-app.html","x":"API Explorer (front end) source code reference"},{"t":"Tests","u":"ref-tests.html","x":"Tests source code reference"},{"t":"Every API route","u":"ref-endpoints.html","x":"every api route endpoint list method path"}]